libopenwch linker script generator
==================================

This directory holds the device database and the generic linker script
template.  Together with scripts/genlink.py they implement the same
"device name in, linker script out" feature that libopencm3 provides.

Files
-----

  devices.data   the device tree (part number -> family group -> ISA)
  linker.ld.S    the generic linker script, preprocessed per device
  tests/         one <part>.data marker and <part>.result expectation per test

How it works
------------

1. The application sets DEVICE, e.g.

	OPENWCH_DIR ?= /path/to/libopenwch
	DEVICE      ?= ch32v003f4p6

	include $(OPENWCH_DIR)/mk/genlink-config.mk

2. genlink-config.mk asks scripts/genlink.py for a series of answers about
   that part number:

	FAMILY     the group that names the archive (libopenwch_ch32v0.a)
	SUBFAMILY  the immediate group (ch32v003)
	MARCH      the -march base ISA (rv32ec)
	ZEXT       the always-on extensions (_zicsr_zifencei)
	MABI       the -mabi (ilp32e)
	ZMMUL      1 if the part needs _zmmul appended to MARCH
	CPPFLAGS   -D<FAMILY> -D<SUBFAMILY> ... -D<PART>
	DEFS       the above plus -D_ROM=... -D_RAM=... -D_ROM_OFF=... etc.

3. mk/genlink-rules.mk preprocesses linker.ld.S with DEFS, producing
   generated.$(DEVICE).ld in the application directory.

The devices.data grammar
------------------------

	<pattern> <parent> (<data> ...)

The query starts by matching <pattern> against the part number (lower-cased,
shell wildcards `? * +`).  Whatever is in the <parent> column becomes the next
query, and traversal continues until a line whose parent is `END`.  A parent of
`+` contributes data without changing the query.

Two rules keep the file consistent:

  1. A part-number pattern must require at least one character after the
     family prefix (`ch32v003?*`, not `ch32v003*`), otherwise it also matches
     its own group name and the traversal stops one level too early.

  2. A group line must be exact (`ch32v0`), so that it does not swallow the
     part numbers that point at it.

Continuation lines use `+` in the parent column and must appear BEFORE the
`END` line, because traversal stops as soon as the `END` line is processed:

	ch32v0          +               ROM_OFF=0x00000000 RAM_OFF=0x20000000
	ch32v0          +               ENTRY=_start GP=1 HICODE=0
	ch32v0          END             ARCH=riscv MARCH=rv32ec MABI=ilp32e

RISC-V specific keys
--------------------

  MARCH=<isa>     base ISA passed to -march
  ZEXT=<exts>     extensions always appended to MARCH
  ZMMUL=1         append _zmmul when the toolchain supports it
  MABI=<abi>      passed to -mabi
  ENTRY=<symbol>  entry point in the generated script
  GP=1            emit __global_pointer$
  HICODE=1        emit the .highcode section

Everything else that matches ^[A-Z0-9_]+$ is passed to the linker script
preprocessor as -D_<KEY>=<VALUE> (note the leading underscore).

Testing
-------

	make genlinktests

runs every ld/tests/*.data marker through scripts/genlinktest.sh, which
compares the full set of query answers against ld/tests/<part>.result.  The
test queries the real devices.data rather than a private copy, so it cannot
silently drift from the device tree.

Adding a family
---------------

  1. Add the lib/<family>/ directory with its Makefile.
  2. Add the part-number line(s) and the group's `+`/`END` lines to
     devices.data.
  3. Add the family to TARGETS in the root Makefile.
  4. Add a ld/tests/<part>.data + .result pair.
